{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Substitution: replace each letter with its value (use brackets for negatives)",
      "Follow BIDMAS to calculate the result",
      "Rearranging: use inverse operations to isolate the new subject",
      "If the subject appears twice, collect, factorise, then divide"
    ],
    "title": "Formulae & Substitution",
    "content": "<p><strong>Substitution</strong> means replacing letters with numbers. Put the number in place of the letter and calculate using BIDMAS.</p><p>Always use brackets when substituting negative numbers: if \\(x = -3\\), then \\(x^2 = (-3)^2 = 9\\), NOT \\(-9\\).</p><p><strong>Rearranging formulae</strong> means making a different variable the subject. Use inverse operations (the same way you solve equations) to isolate the required variable. If the subject appears in a fraction, multiply both sides to clear it. If it appears under a square root, square both sides.</p><p>When the new subject appears <em>more than once</em>, collect all terms containing it on one side, factor it out, then divide.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>Given</strong> \\(y = 3x^2 - 5\\), find \\(y\\) when \\(x = -2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Substitute: \\(y = 3(-2)^2 - 5\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(= 3(4) - 5 = 12 - 5 = 7\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(y = 7\\)</p>"
  },
